/* Figure walkthroughs for six companies whose figures were explained in plain language before lessons existed.
   Each one reads the company's verified financial record and explains it; the short page shows it under "How to read these figures".
   Text and calculations are unchanged from the original company pages. */
window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS={};
(()=>{
 const id='hindustan-unilever';
 const base='https://hul-performance-highlights.hul.co.in/performance-highlights-fy-2025-2026/segments/';
 const annual='https://www.hul.co.in/files/annual-report-2025-26-single-page.pdf';
 const esc=v=>String(v).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 const ref=(url,label)=>`<a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>`;
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note">${ref(url,label)}${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],home=f.rows.find(r=>r.name==='Home Care'),beauty=f.rows.find(r=>r.name==='Beauty & Wellbeing');return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>Read these HUL figures in ordinary rupees</h3><p>For every ₹100 of reported segment sales, Home Care generated about <strong>₹${(home.profit/home.revenue*100).toFixed(2)}</strong> of segment result, compared with about <strong>₹${(beauty.profit/beauty.revenue*100).toFixed(2)}</strong> for Beauty & Wellbeing.</p><p>These are calculated as segment result ÷ segment revenue × 100 from the FY 2025–26 table above. They explain why a bigger business by sales can have a lower margin. They are <strong>not</strong> profit after tax, profit on a ₹100 retail pack, or returns you would earn on an HUL share.</p><p>Also keep the scope straight: this table is consolidated and covers continuing operations. It excludes the discontinued ice-cream business. Do not mix it with a standalone headline or assume the “Others” row is an unlisted brand’s profit.</p><details class="primer-detail"><summary>What to check next in HUL’s own accounts</summary><ul><li>Operating cash flow: did business operations generate cash, and how do inventory and customer/supplier payments explain any gap from profit?</li><li>Investment spending: what money went into facilities, technology or acquisitions?</li><li>Balance sheet and related-party notes: what obligations, cash holdings and group payments need understanding?</li><li>Share price and valuation: what expectations does today’s price assume? The business explanation alone does not answer this.</li></ul></details>${note(f.source,'Source of the calculation',esc(f.location))}</div>`}
 window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS[id]=numbers;
})();
(()=>{
 const id='itc';
 const root='https://itcportal.com/',business=root+'itc-businesses/';
 const urls={profile:root+'about-itc/our-profile.html',fmcg:business+'fmcg.html',food:business+'fmcg/food.html',cigarettes:business+'fmcg/cigarette.html',agri:business+'agri-business.html',paper:business+'paperboards-and-specialty-papers.html',packaging:business+'packaging.html',technology:business+'Information-Technology.html',group:business+'group-companies.html'};
 const source=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id].source;
 const esc=v=>String(v).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note"><a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],cig=f.rows.find(r=>r.name==='FMCG – Cigarettes'),other=f.rows.find(r=>r.name==='FMCG – Other products');return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>What these ITC figures mean in plain language</h3><p>Of every ₹100 of the <strong>combined reported segment result</strong>, approximately <strong>₹${(cig.profit/f.profitTotal*100).toFixed(2)}</strong> came from cigarettes and <strong>₹${(other.profit/f.profitTotal*100).toFixed(2)}</strong> from other FMCG products in this reporting year.</p><p>Calculated as the relevant segment result ÷ the reported segment-result total × 100. The total includes the loss in Other businesses. These are contributions to segment earnings, <strong>not product margins, profit after tax or a shareholder’s return</strong>. They explain why ITC cannot be understood from a food-brand shopping list alone.</p><p><strong>Read the two “other” labels separately:</strong> FMCG – Other products is the consumer-goods bundle that includes foods, personal care and stationery. Other businesses is a separate reported row.</p><details class="primer-detail"><summary>Why the sales rows do not add up to outside sales</summary><p>ITC reports ₹90,713 crore of total segment revenue, then deducts ₹9,846 crore of inter-segment revenue to reach ₹80,867 crore of gross revenue. Internal activity is removed to avoid counting it as another outside sale. Independently rounded segment rows differ from the reported total by ₹1 crore.</p></details><details class="primer-detail"><summary>Why cigarette revenue needs an extra check</summary><p>The presentation says cigarette taxation changed from 1 February 2026. Gross revenue and excise duties for the quarter and full year are therefore not strictly comparable with the previous year. Do not read the gross revenue growth rate as if it were the same as quantity growth or profit growth. This page does not estimate how much each tax change altered demand.</p></details><details class="primer-detail"><summary>What this standalone table leaves out</summary><p>This is the parent company’s segment statement. It does not show ITC Infotech’s sales as a separate technology business, and its Other businesses row is not a proxy for that subsidiary. Consult consolidated reporting and the relevant subsidiary accounts for the wider group. Company profit also includes reconciling items beyond the segment result.</p></details><p>For the next financial checks, read operating cash flow, stock and customer-payment balances, investment spending and acquisition notes. Those checks help connect the earnings story to the cash needed to run and expand the business.</p>${note(f.source,'Source for the figures and calculations',esc(f.location))}</div>`}
 window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS[id]=numbers;
})();
(()=>{
 const id='nestle-india';
 const root='https://www.nestle.in/';
 const urls={company:root+'about-us/at-a-glance',brands:root+'info/sitemap',annual:root+'sites/g/files/pydnoa451/files/2026-06/AnnualReport2025-26andAGMNotice.pdf',figures:root+'investors/keyfigures',foods:root+'brands/foods',coffee:root+'brands/coffee',milk:root+'brands/dairy',nutrition:root+'brands/nutrition',chocolate:root+'brands/chocolatesandconfectionery',professional:root+'media/pressreleases/allpressreleases/nestl%C3%A9-professional-reaches-landmark-1000th-retail-one-kiosk-india',nespresso:root+'sites/g/files/pydnoa451/files/2025-03/NSEBSESTXintimationNespressosigned.pdf',royalty:root+'investors/stockandfinancials/royalty'};
 const esc=v=>String(v).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note"><a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],p=f.productSales,fmt=n=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-IN',{minimumFractionDigits:2,maximumFractionDigits:2}).format(n);return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>Which product groups bring in sales?</h3><p>This is <strong>sales only</strong> for FY 2025–26, on the standalone India basis. Category and brand profits are not supplied by this table.</p><table class="financial-table"><caption class="sr-only">Nestlé India standalone product-group sales, FY 2025–26. No category profit allocation.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Product group</th><th scope="col">Sales<br><small>₹ crore</small></th><th scope="col">Share of product sales<br><small>% · calculated</small></th></tr></thead><tbody>${p.rows.map(r=>`<tr><th scope="row">${esc(r.name)}</th><td>${fmt(r.revenue)}</td><td>${(r.revenue/p.total*100).toFixed(2)}%</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Sale of products</th><td>${fmt(p.total)}</td><td>100%</td></tr></tfoot></table><p>Milk Products and Nutrition has the most reported product sales in this table. That does not establish which group earns the most profit, because separate group profits are not disclosed in the cited segment note.</p><p>Product sales of ₹${fmt(p.total)} crore plus other operating revenue of ₹${fmt(p.otherOperatingRevenue)} crore give the company’s revenue from operations of ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].revenue)} crore above. Sales shares are calculated as group sales ÷ total product sales × 100 and rounded for display. Independently rounded percentages may not add to exactly 100%. They are not shares of profit.</p>${note(p.source,'Official annual report: source of product sales and definitions',esc(p.location))}<h3>Read the company profit without assigning it to a brand</h3><p>The overall ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].profit)} crore profit after tax is a company result. It includes the reported expenses, exceptional items and tax. Dividing it among MAGGI, NESCAFÉ, KITKAT or these product groups in proportion to their sales would invent a profit breakdown.</p><details class="primer-detail"><summary>Why historical comparisons need a period check</summary><p>FY 2025–26 covers 12 months ended 31 March 2026. Nestlé India’s transition year 2023–24 covered 15 months, so its raw sales or profit totals should not be compared with a 12-month year as if the periods were equal. The company explains this in its key-figures footnote.</p>${note(urls.figures,'Official period-comparability note')}</details><p>Next, read operating cash flow, inventory, customer and supplier balances, factory investment and the related-party notes. Those checks connect the earnings total to the cash needed to run and expand the business.</p></div>`}
 window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS[id]=numbers;
})();
(()=>{
 const id='britannia';
 const urls={company:'https://www.britannia.co.in/about-us',products:'https://www.britannia.co.in/',annual:'https://media.britannia.co.in/Britannia_Annual_Report_2025_26_High_Resolution_c738982760.pdf',investors:'https://www.britannia.co.in/investors/financial-performance/annual-report',cheese:'https://media.britannia.co.in/Press_Release_09_10_2024_747d4ea5a0.pdf'};
 const esc=v=>String(v).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note"><a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],p=f.context,fmt=n=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-IN',{minimumFractionDigits:2,maximumFractionDigits:2}).format(n);return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>What can the product mix tell us?</h3><p>The FY 2025–26 business responsibility report gives the approximate turnover mix below. It groups bakery and salted snacks together; <strong>it is not a biscuit-only sales split or a profit breakdown</strong>. The report states a consolidated reporting boundary unless otherwise specified.</p><table class="financial-table"><caption class="sr-only">Britannia FY 2025–26 approximate turnover mix, BRSR. No category profit allocation.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Reported product grouping</th><th scope="col">Approximate share of turnover<br><small>Reported · %</small></th></tr></thead><tbody>${p.turnoverMix.map(r=>`<tr><th scope="row">${esc(r.name)}</th><td>~${r.share}%</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table><p>Bakery and salted snacks dominate this mix. The table does not tell us the separate sales or profit of Good Day, Marie Gold, cheese or any other brand. Multiplying rounded shares by group revenue would not create an exact category-sales disclosure.</p>${note(f.source,'Official turnover-mix source','BRSR · item 13 reporting boundary and item 17 products/services · printed pages 107–108')}
 <details class="primer-detail"><summary>Where does reported group revenue come from?</summary><p>Consolidated Note 43 reports revenue by geographical market, including other operating revenue. These figures cover the same period and total as the company revenue above; they do not give separate geographical profits.</p><table class="financial-table"><caption class="sr-only">Britannia consolidated revenue by geographical market, FY 2025–26.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Geographical market</th><th scope="col">Revenue<br><small>₹ crore</small></th></tr></thead><tbody>${p.geographies.map(r=>`<tr><th scope="row">${esc(r.name)}</th><td>${fmt(r.revenue)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Total revenue from operations</th><td>${fmt(f.rows[0].revenue)}</td></tr></tfoot></table>${note(f.source,'Official geography disclosure','Consolidated Note 43 · printed page 309')}</details>
 <h3>Read the sales and profit definitions carefully</h3><p>Sale of goods of ₹${fmt(p.goods)} crore plus other operating revenue of ₹${fmt(p.otherOperating)} crore gives revenue from operations of ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].revenue)} crore. Other income is separate. The report’s other operating revenue includes items such as scrap sales, royalty income and government incentives; it is not all biscuit sales.</p><p>Group profit after tax is ₹${fmt(p.groupPat)} crore. Of that, ₹${fmt(p.nonControlling)} crore belongs to non-controlling interests, leaving ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].profit)} crore attributable to the owners of Britannia, the figure in the main table. These are different labelled measures, not conflicting versions of the same number.</p>${note(f.source,'Official sales and profit reconciliation','Consolidated statement of profit and loss · printed page 261; Notes 29 and 53')}
 <details class="primer-detail"><summary>What do the accounts show about the cheese joint venture?</summary><p>For FY 2025–26, Note 7 reports Britannia Bel Foods’ loss for the year of ₹${fmt(p.jointVenture.loss)} crore and Britannia’s ${p.jointVenture.interest}% interest. The consolidated related-party note records a share of loss in that joint venture of <strong>−₹${fmt(p.jointVenture.shareOfLoss)} crore</strong>.</p><p>This is an entity-level joint-venture result, not a profit margin for a cheese pack or a result for every dairy product. The equity-method share of loss affects group earnings; the venture’s full sales are not simply added to consolidated revenue.</p>${note(f.source,'Official joint-venture disclosures','Consolidated Notes 7 and 45 · printed pages 284 and 313')}</details><p>Next, read operating cash flow, inventory, customer and supplier balances, capital spending, debt and dividends. Those checks help establish whether reported earnings translate into cash and how that cash is used.</p></div>`}
 window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS[id]=numbers;
})();
(()=>{
 const id='marico';
 const urls={brands:'https://marico.com/india/brands',company:'https://marico.com/india/about-us',annual:'https://marico.com/investorspdf/Marico_Annual_Report_FY26.pdf',reports:'https://marico.com/india/investors/annual-report',shampoo:'https://marico.com/global/media/detail/what_makes_heads_turn_the_newly_launched_parachute_advansed_protein_shampoo_reveals_the_answer_in_its_first_campaign_film_aisa_kya_hai?id=260&type=press'};
 const esc=v=>String(v).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note"><a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],p=f.productRevenue,fmt=n=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-IN',{minimumFractionDigits:2,maximumFractionDigits:2}).format(n),india=f.rows.find(r=>r.name==='India'),international=f.rows.find(r=>r.name==='International');return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>Which product categories generate revenue?</h3><p>This FY 2025–26 consolidated disclosure shows <strong>product-category revenue only</strong>. The labels below are those in the annual report; they are not a separate profit statement for Saffola, Parachute or any other brand.</p><table class="financial-table"><caption class="sr-only">Marico consolidated product-category revenue, FY 2025–26. No category profit allocation.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Reported product category</th><th scope="col">Revenue<br><small>₹ crore</small></th><th scope="col">Share of total<br><small>% · calculated</small></th></tr></thead><tbody>${p.rows.map(r=>`<tr><th scope="row">${esc(r.name)}</th><td>${fmt(r.revenue)}</td><td>${(r.revenue/p.total*100).toFixed(2)}%</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">Reported total</th><td>${fmt(p.total)}</td><td>100%</td></tr></tfoot></table><p>The category rows and the India/International rows are <strong>two views of the same group revenue</strong>. Do not add the two tables together. The annual report does not provide a matching separate profit for each of these product categories in this note.</p><p>Shares are calculated as category revenue ÷ reported total × 100. Rounded percentages may not add to exactly 100%. A revenue share is not a profit share.</p>${note(p.source,'Official product-category revenue disclosure',esc(p.location))}
 <h3>More sales and a higher result per sale are different</h3><p>India has both more revenue and more reported segment profit than International in the main table. For each ₹100 of segment revenue, the segment result is approximately <strong>₹${(india.profit/india.revenue*100).toFixed(2)} for India</strong> and <strong>₹${(international.profit/international.revenue*100).toFixed(2)} for International</strong>.</p><p>Calculated as reported segment result ÷ the corresponding segment revenue × 100. These are calculated segment-result-to-sales ratios, not a brand margin, the company’s separately defined EBITDA margin, profit after tax or a shareholder return. Different products and markets help explain why the ratios can differ.</p>
 <details class="primer-detail"><summary>How does segment result become company profit?</summary><p>The combined ₹${fmt(f.profitTotal)} crore segment result is reduced by ₹${fmt(p.financeCosts)} crore of finance costs and ₹${fmt(p.unallocatedNetExpense)} crore of net unallocated expenditure to reach ₹${fmt(p.pbt)} crore profit before tax.</p><p>Tax of ₹${fmt(p.tax)} crore leaves ₹${fmt(p.groupPat)} crore group profit after tax. After ₹${fmt(p.nonControlling)} crore belonging to non-controlling interests, profit attributable to owners is ₹${fmt(p.ownersPat)} crore. These are successive, differently defined measures. The segment-result total should not be called net profit.</p>${note(p.source,'Official reconciliation and consolidated results','Note 30 · printed pages 434–435; management review · printed page 234')}</details><p>For the next checks, read operating cash flow, inventory, customer and supplier balances, factory spending and acquisition payments. Neither these earnings figures nor a brand’s popularity establish whether the current share price offers good value.</p></div>`}
 window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS[id]=numbers;
})();
(()=>{
 const id='avenue-supermarts';
 const urls={company:'https://www.dmartindia.com/about-us',ready:'https://www.dmart.in/',results:'https://api.dmartindia.com/corporate/content/file/v1/2/jZtJ4gV9JIDY9CjsdXXbQxhE1777722483/Outcome%20of%20the%20Board%20Meeting%20held%20on%20May%202%2C%202026.pdf',release:'https://api.dmartindia.com/corporate/content/file/v1/2/R2aWBiIpiuD39xgfm4wrqQKc1777723315/Press%20release%20dated%202nd%20May%2C%202026.pdf',investors:'https://www.dmartindia.com/investor-relations/financial-results'};
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note"><a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],p=f.retailContext,fmt=n=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-IN',{minimumFractionDigits:2,maximumFractionDigits:2}).format(n);return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>Connect a large sales total to what remains</h3><p>For every ₹100 of consolidated revenue from operations, approximately <strong>₹${(f.rows[0].profit/f.rows[0].revenue*100).toFixed(2)}</strong> remained as group profit after tax in FY 2025–26. Calculated as ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].profit)} crore ÷ ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].revenue)} crore × 100.</p><p>This is a company-level profit-to-revenue ratio, not a gross retail mark-up, an individual store’s margin, cash available after expansion or a shareholder return. A small change in costs can matter when only a limited amount remains from each sale.</p><details class="primer-detail"><summary>Why standalone and consolidated totals differ</summary><p>The parent’s FY 2025–26 standalone revenue from operations was ₹${fmt(p.parentRevenue)} crore and profit after tax was ₹${fmt(p.parentPat)} crore. The main table shows the wider group, including subsidiaries and consolidation adjustments.</p><p>Do not add these overlapping totals, or call their difference DMart Ready’s result. The group includes more than that subsidiary. Use Avenue E-Commerce’s own accounts to study the online business.</p>${note(f.source,'Official parent and consolidated statements','PDF pages 6 and 14; consolidated subsidiary list and reporting notes')}</details>
 <details class="primer-detail"><summary>What does the reported store-growth comparison measure?</summary><p>The May 2026 release reports ${p.existingStoreGrowth}% growth for stores two years and older during the quarter ended 31 March 2026. This is a specific quarterly store set, not full-year growth for the entire chain or a direct measure of quantity consumed.</p><p>The release also reports ${p.storeCount} stores as of 31 March 2026, explicitly including one store temporarily closed to customers for reconstruction. Store count is therefore not a count of equally productive, fully operating locations.</p>${note(p.source,'Official store-age definition and count qualification','May 2026 release · PDF page 4')}</details><p>The cited audited results report retail trades through offline and online channels with no separate reportable segments. They do not give a separate profit for groceries, apparel or each store. Do not allocate group profit to shelf categories in proportion to sales.</p><p>Next, compare operating cash flow with stock needs, property and fit-out spending, borrowings and lease obligations. A business can report profit while spending more cash to open and develop stores.</p></div>`}
 window.FIGURE_WALKTHROUGHS[id]=numbers;
})();
